## Tool Class — `ApiTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Fetches a user by ID from the REST API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The numeric user ID to look up")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Lists all posts for a given user.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUserPosts(
        [Description("The user ID whose posts to retrieve")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var json = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/{userId}/posts", cancellationToken);
        return json;
    }
}
```

## `Program.cs` — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient();  // registers IHttpClientFactory + HttpClient

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK integrates directly with `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection`:

1. **Constructor injection** — Make your tool class non-static with a primary constructor. Services like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, or your own interfaces are injected when the class is instantiated.

2. **Method parameter injection** — For static tool classes, DI services can appear as method parameters. The SDK resolves them at invocation time.

3. **What's hidden from the schema** — `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<T>`, and any DI-registered service are **automatically excluded** from the tool's JSON schema. Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed to the LLM.

4. **Registration** — Call `.WithToolsFromAssembly()` to auto-discover all `[McpServerToolType]` classes, or `.WithTools<ApiTools>()` for explicit registration (required for Native AOT).

**Key takeaway:** The DI container owns the tool class lifetime. You register services normally in `Program.cs` (`AddHttpClient()`, `AddSingleton<T>()`, etc.), and the MCP SDK wires them into your tools automatically — no factories or service locators needed.
